<p>Restaurants are brilliant at the in-person experience. The food, the service, the atmosphere, the little details that make a meal feel special. But the moment a customer walks out the door, most restaurants go completely silent. No follow-up, no thank you, no reason to come back. The relationship ends at the receipt.</p>

<p>That is a massive missed opportunity. Research consistently shows that repeat customers spend around 67% more than first-time visitors. They are also far cheaper to retain than new diners are to acquire. The restaurants that thrive long-term are not just the ones with the best menu. They are the ones that stay connected with their customers between visits.</p>

<p>The good news is that you do not need extra staff or a dedicated marketing team to make this happen. With the right <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">automation workflows</a>, your restaurant can follow up with every single guest, request reviews at the perfect moment, celebrate birthdays, reward loyalty, and fill tables during quiet periods. All without anyone on your team having to remember to press send.</p>

<p>Here is how it works in practice.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Reservation reminders and no-show reduction</h2>

<p>No-shows are one of the most frustrating problems in the restaurant industry. Depending on the venue, they can cost between 5% and 20% of total revenue. A table sitting empty on a Friday night because someone forgot their booking is money you cannot recover. You turned away other guests for that reservation.</p>

<p>Automated <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS reminders</a> are one of the simplest and most effective fixes available. The workflow is straightforward: when a customer books a table, they enter your system. From there, two messages go out automatically.</p>

<p><strong>24 hours before the reservation:</strong></p>
<p>"Hi Sarah, just a reminder that your table for 4 at Riverstone Kitchen is booked for tomorrow at 7:30pm. Reply Y to confirm or call us on 03 9555 1234 to reschedule."</p>

<p><strong>2 hours before the reservation:</strong></p>
<p>"Your table at Riverstone Kitchen is ready for you tonight at 7:30pm. Running late? No worries. Just reply to this message and we will hold your table."</p>

<p>That confirmation request in the first message is important. When someone replies Y, they are making a small commitment. Psychologically, that makes them far less likely to simply not show up. And if they need to cancel, the reminder gives them a chance to do it early enough for you to fill the table.</p>

<p>Restaurants that implement automated reservation reminders typically see no-show rates drop by 30% to 50%. For a venue doing 100 covers on a busy night, that could mean recovering 5 to 10 tables that would have otherwise sat empty. You can learn more about this in our guide to <a href="/blog/reduce-no-shows">reducing no-shows with automation</a>.</p>

<blockquote><p>A simple two-message reminder sequence can cut your no-show rate in half. That is not a marketing tactic. It is revenue protection.</p></blockquote>

