<h2>Post-visit review requests</h2>

<p>Online reviews are the lifeblood of restaurant marketing. When someone searches for "Italian restaurant near me" or "best brunch in Melbourne," Google is showing them results ranked heavily by review count and rating. Restaurants with 100 or more Google reviews consistently outperform competitors in local search results. The data on this is clear.</p>

<p>The problem is that most happy customers never leave a review unless you ask them. And the window for asking is narrow. If you wait a week, they have moved on. If you ask at the table, it feels awkward and they rarely follow through. The sweet spot is the morning after their visit.</p>

<p>An automated review request sent at 10am the next day catches people during their morning routine. They are checking their phone, they remember the meal, and a quick tap on a direct link makes it easy to leave a few words.</p>

<p>Here is what that message looks like:</p>

<p>"Thanks for dining with us last night, Sarah. We loved having you. If you enjoyed your experience, a quick Google review would mean the world to us: [direct link]. It only takes 30 seconds."</p>

<p>A few things make this work. First, the personalisation. Using their name and referencing "last night" makes it feel like a genuine message, not a mass blast. Second, the direct link. Do not send people to your Google Business page and hope they figure it out. Give them a link that opens the review form directly. Third, the low-pressure tone. "Would mean the world" is warm without being pushy.</p>

<p>For a deeper look at building a review generation system, check out our full guide on <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">getting more Google reviews</a>. Combined with a broader <a href="/blog/reputation-management">reputation management strategy</a>, automated review requests can transform your online presence within a few months.</p>

